    '# Document Metadata
Title: Getting Gender Right in Neural Machine Translation
Authors: Eva Vanmassenhove, Christian Hardmeier, Andy Way
Date: October 31 - November 4, 2018
Venue: Proceedings of the 2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing
Pages: 6

# Abstract
Speakers of different languages must attend to and encode strikingly different aspects of the world in order to use their language correctly (Sapir, 1921; Slobin, 1996). One such difference is related to the way gender is expressed in a language. Saying “I am happy” in English, does not encode any additional knowledge of the speaker that uttered the sentence. However, many other languages do have grammatical gender systems and so such knowledge would be encoded. In order to correctly translate s

# Section Summaries

## 1. Abstract (pages 1-1)
**Overview:** The abstract discusses the importance of gender in language and its implications for Neural Machine Translation (NMT). It highlights the differences in gender expression across languages and presents the authors'' contributions to improving translation quality by integrating gender information into NMT systems.
**Key Topics:** Gender in language, Neural Machine Translation (NMT), Dataset compilation for language pairs
**Key Metrics:** language_pairs: 20; experiments_conducted: multiple
- Incorporating gender information into NMT significantly improves translation quality for some language pairs.
- Current MT systems often perpetuate male bias, leading to negative discrimination.
**Notable Facts:**
- The study references foundational works by Sapir (1921) and Slobin (1996) on language and gender.
- Errors in gender-related translations can harm fluency and politeness in communication.

## 2. 1 Introduction (pages 1-1)
**Overview:** This section introduces the challenges and importance of incorporating gender information in Neural Machine Translation (NMT). It highlights the differences in language use between genders and discusses how these differences affect translation quality, particularly in languages with grammatical gender systems.
**Key Topics:** Gender in language and translation, Neural Machine Translation (NMT), Author profiling and gender classification
**Key Metrics:** language_pairs: 20; experiments_conducted: multiple
- Adding gender features to NMT systems significantly improves translation quality for some language pairs.
- Current MT systems often perpetuate a male bias, leading to negative discrimination.
**Notable Facts:**
- The study references empirical and theoretical work on gender differences in language by Coates (2015).
- Human translators use contextual information to infer gender, which current MT systems fail to do.

## 3. 2 Related Work (pages 2-2)
**Overview:** This section discusses the related work in the field of machine translation (MT) focusing on the preservation of author traits, particularly gender, in neural machine translation (NMT) systems. It highlights previous studies that have explored gender differences in language and the challenges faced in integrating these traits into MT systems, setting the stage for the authors'' novel approach to building a speaker-informed NMT system.
**Key Topics:** Gender differences in language, Personalized machine translation, Domain-adaptation techniques in MT
**Key Metrics:** number_of_studies_reviewed: 3; language_pairs_with_annotated_datasets: 20; annotated_sentences_in_EN-FR_and_EN-DE: not specified
- Mirkin et al. (2015) found that MT does not preserve original author traits, suggesting domain-adaptation techniques for personalization.
- Rabinovich et al. (2017) attempted to preserve gender traits in SMT but did not achieve improvements over baseline models.
- The authors claim their work is the first to build a speaker-informed NMT system, drawing parallels to Sennrich et al. (2016) on controlling politeness.
**Notable Facts:**
- Rabinovich et al. (2017) published an annotated parallel dataset for English-French (EN-FR) and English-German (EN-DE) language pairs.
- The authors published a collection of parallel corpora under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License for 20 language pairs.

## 4. 3 Compilation of Datasets (pages 3-3)
**Overview:** This section presents a compilation of datasets used for training neural machine translation (NMT) systems, focusing on the Europarl corpus. It details the number of annotated parallel sentences available for various language pairs and analyzes the gender distribution of speakers within the dataset, highlighting potential biases in the data.
**Key Topics:** Dataset Compilation, Gender Distribution in Speech, NMT System Training
**Key Metrics:** total_sentences_EN_BG: 306380; total_sentences_EN_IT: 1297635; percentage_female_speakers: 32.5; percentage_male_speakers: 67.39; percentage_sentences_by_age_group_50_60: 43.76
- The Europarl dataset shows a gender imbalance with 67.39% of sentences produced by male speakers.
- In the EN-FR subcorpus, the youngest age group (20-30) contributes only 0.71% of the total sentences.
**Notable Facts:**
- The dataset includes over 1.4 million sentences for language pairs such as EN-ES and EN-PT.
- The analysis indicates that the gender imbalance in the dataset may influence the translations produced by NMT systems.

## 5. 4 Experimental Setup (pages 4-4)
**Overview:** This section details the experimental setup for testing gender-enhanced neural machine translation (NMT) systems across various languages. It compares the performance of these systems against baseline models using BLEU scores, particularly focusing on how gender tagging affects translation accuracy.
**Key Topics:** Gender-enhanced NMT systems, BLEU score comparison, Performance across different languages
**Key Metrics:** BLEU score improvement (DA): +0.31; BLEU score for French (EN-TAG): 39.26; BLEU score for Spanish (EN-TAG): 42.28; Statistical significance threshold: p < 0.05
- Significant improvements in BLEU scores for gender-enhanced systems were observed for French, Italian, Portuguese, and Greek.
- The Spanish gender-enhanced system showed a slight decrease in BLEU score (-0.19).
- The Danish system achieved a significant improvement, while Dutch, German, Finnish, and Swedish systems did not show improvements.
**Notable Facts:**
- The biggest BLEU score improvement was noted on female test sets, particularly for first-person singular pronouns.
- In specific cases, the gender-informed system correctly used female forms in translations where the baseline system failed.

## 6. 5 Results (pages 5-5)
**Overview:** This section presents the results of an experiment that incorporated speaker-gender tags into neural machine translation (NMT) systems to enhance morphological agreement and word choice accuracy. It discusses the impact of gender on language use, particularly in French, and highlights the differences in word preferences between male and female speakers.
**Key Topics:** Gender-informed translation systems, Word choice differences by gender, Morphological agreement in NMT
**Key Metrics:** male_word_frequency_position_crois: 303; female_word_frequency_position_crois: 373; male_word_frequency_position_pense: 151; female_word_frequency_position_pense: 153
- The gender-informed system showed improved performance over the baseline system, particularly in languages with grammatical gender.
- The verb ''croire'' is used more frequently by males, indicating a tendency towards more assertive speech compared to females who prefer ''penser''.
**Notable Facts:**
- The study aligns with previous corpus linguistic studies indicating that gender influences not only morphological agreement but also lexical choices.
- Future research aims to analyze more language pairs and explore additional methods for integrating speaker information.

## 7. 6 Conclusions and Future Work (pages 6-6)
**Overview:** This section presents a collection of references and citations related to various aspects of machine translation, language use, and author profiling. It highlights significant contributions from multiple authors and studies, showcasing advancements in statistical and neural machine translation, as well as sociolinguistic analyses of language differences.
**Key Topics:** Machine Translation Techniques, Sociolinguistic Gender Differences, Author Profiling and Personalization
**Key Metrics:** number_of_references: 30; years_of_publication: 1921-2018
- The use of RNN Encoder-Decoder models for statistical machine translation was highlighted in a 2014 study by Cho et al.
- The BLEU metric for automatic evaluation of machine translation was introduced by Papineni et al. in 2002.
**Notable Facts:**
- The Europarl corpus, a significant resource for statistical machine translation, was discussed by Koehn in 2005.
- Google''s Multilingual Neural Machine Translation System, enabling zero-shot translation, was detailed by Johnson et al. in 2017.

## 8. Acknowledgements (pages 6-6)
**Overview:** The Acknowledgements section lists various influential works and authors in the field of machine translation and sociolinguistics, highlighting significant contributions to the understanding of language processing, gender differences in language use, and advancements in neural machine translation. This section serves to recognize the foundational research that informs the current study.
**Key Topics:** Machine Translation, Sociolinguistics, Gender Differences in Language
**Key Metrics:** total_references: 30; years_covered: 1921-2018
- The section references seminal works such as BLEU for machine translation evaluation (Papineni et al., 2002) and sequence-to-sequence learning (Sutskever et al., 2014).
- It includes studies on gender differences in language use, such as the analysis of 14,000 text samples (Newman et al., 2008).
**Notable Facts:**
- The earliest reference is from Edward Sapir in 1921, discussing the study of speech.
- Recent contributions include the 2018 work by Vanmassenhove and Hardmeier on Europarl datasets with demographic speaker information.

## 9. References (pages 6-6)
**Overview:** This section lists references cited throughout the document, primarily focusing on research related to machine translation, gender differences in language, and author profiling. It includes a variety of sources from conference proceedings, journals, and books, highlighting significant contributions to the fields of computational linguistics and natural language processing.
**Key Topics:** Machine Translation, Gender Differences in Language, Author Profiling
**Key Metrics:** total_references: 30; years_covered: 1921-2018
- The references include seminal works such as ''BLEU: A Method for Automatic Evaluation of Machine Translation'' by Kishore Papineni et al. (2002) and ''Learning Phrase Representations using RNN Encoder–Decoder for Statistical Machine Translation'' by Kyunghyun Cho et al. (2014).
- Research on gender differences in language is represented by multiple studies, including ''Gender Differences in Language Use: An Analysis of 14,000 Text Samples'' by Matthew L Newman et al. (2008).
**Notable Facts:**
- The earliest reference is Edward Sapir''s book ''Language: An Introduction to the Study of Speech'' published in 1921.
- The most recent reference is from Eva Vanmassenhove and Christian Hardmeier in 2018, discussing Europarl datasets with demographic speaker information.
