    '# Document Metadata
Title: Evaluating Gender Bias in Machine Translation
Authors: Gabriel Stanovsky, Noah A. Smith, Luke Zettlemoyer
Date: July 28 - August 2, 2019
Venue: Proceedings of the 57th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics
Pages: 6

# Abstract
The doctor asked the nurse to help her in the procedure

We present the first challenge set and evaluation protocol for the analysis of gender bias in machine translation (MT). Our approach uses two recent coreference resolution datasets composed of English sentences which cast participants into non-stereotypical gender roles (e.g., “The doctor asked the nurse to help _her_ in the operation”). We devise an automatic gender bias evaluation method for eight target languages with grammatical gender

# Section Summaries

## 1. Abstract (pages 1-1)
**Overview:** This section presents the first challenge set and evaluation protocol for analyzing gender bias in machine translation (MT). It discusses the methodology used to assess gender bias across eight target languages, highlighting significant biases found in popular MT systems and academic models.
**Key Topics:** Gender bias in machine translation, Coreference resolution datasets, Evaluation protocol for MT systems
**Key Metrics:** number_of_languages_tested: 8; number_of_MT_systems_evaluated: 6; human_agreement_on_coreference: 95%
- Four popular industrial MT systems and two state-of-the-art academic models exhibit significant gender-biased translation errors.
- The evaluation utilized datasets from recent coreference gender-bias studies, Winogender and WinoBias.
**Notable Facts:**
- The study uses English sentences that place participants in non-stereotypical gender roles.
- The authors'' data and code are publicly available at https://github.com/gabrielStanovsky/mt_gender.

## 2. 1 Introduction (pages 1-1)
**Overview:** This section introduces the evaluation of gender bias in machine translation (MT), presenting the first challenge set and evaluation protocol aimed at analyzing such biases across multiple languages. It highlights the prevalence of gender stereotypes in MT systems and outlines the methodology used for the evaluation, including the datasets and automatic evaluation methods employed.
**Key Topics:** Gender bias in machine translation, Coreference resolution datasets, Evaluation protocol for gender bias
**Key Metrics:** number_of_languages_tested: 8; number_of_MT_systems_evaluated: 6; human_agreement_on_coreference_resolution: 95%
- Popular MT systems exhibit significant gender-biased translation errors across all tested languages.
- Coreference resolution models often ignore context, leading to biased predictions based on stereotypes.
**Notable Facts:**
- The datasets used for evaluation include Winogender and WinoBias, which focus on non-stereotypical gender roles.
- Google Translate has attempted to mitigate biases by allowing users to choose between gendered translations.

## 3. 2 Challenge Set for Gender Bias in MT (pages 2-2)
**Overview:** This section outlines the creation of a challenge set, named WinoMT, to evaluate gender bias in machine translation (MT) systems. It details the methodology for assessing gender bias through a concatenation of existing coreference test sets, Winogender and WinoBias, and describes the process of translating and analyzing gendered entities across multiple languages.
**Key Topics:** Gender bias in machine translation, WinoMT challenge set, Evaluation methodology for MT systems
**Key Metrics:** total_instances_in_WinoMT: 3888; male_instances: 1826; female_instances: 1822; neutral_instances: 240
- Four widely used commercial MT systems and two state-of-the-art academic models exhibit significant gender bias across all tested languages.
- The WinoMT dataset is balanced between male and female genders and includes both stereotypical and non-stereotypical gender roles.
**Notable Facts:**
- WinoMT consists of 3,888 instances, with an equal distribution of male and female gender roles.
- The evaluation process includes automatic translation, alignment, and morphological analysis to extract gender information from translations.

## 4. 3 Evaluation (pages 3-3)
**Overview:** This section evaluates the performance of various commercial machine translation (MT) systems on gender accuracy across multiple languages. It presents findings from the WinoMT corpus, highlighting the systems'' biases in translating gendered nouns and their overall accuracy in preserving gender from English to target languages.
**Key Topics:** Machine Translation Systems Evaluation, Gender Accuracy in Translation, Morphological Analysis of Languages
**Key Metrics:** Accuracy: Best performing model accuracy ranges from 39.6% to 74.1% across languages; Delta_G: Difference in performance between masculine and feminine translations; Delta_S: Difference in performance between pro-stereotypical and anti-stereotypical gender role assignments
- All tested MT systems exhibit gender bias, with most performing poorly on gender accuracy.
- The best performing model for each language often does not exceed random guessing, except for German where three out of four systems performed better.
- All systems, except Microsoft Translator on German, show better performance on male roles, likely due to training set biases.
**Notable Facts:**
- Spanish (ES) had the highest accuracy for Google Translate at 53.1%, while German (DE) had the highest at 74.1% for the same system.
- Amazon Translate does not have a trained model for English to Ukrainian.
- The languages evaluated include Spanish, French, Italian, Russian, Ukrainian, Hebrew, Arabic, and German, representing four different language families.

## 5. 4 Discussion (pages 4-4)
**Overview:** This section discusses the performance of various machine translation (MT) systems, particularly Google Translate, in translating gender roles from the WinoMT corpus. It highlights the disparity in accuracy between stereotypical and non-stereotypical gender role translations and explores methods to mitigate bias in translations.
**Key Topics:** Gender bias in machine translation, Performance comparison of MT systems, Impact of stereotypical adjectives on translation accuracy
**Key Metrics:** Google Translate accuracy on stereotypical roles: 53.1% (Spanish), 37.7% (Russian), 38.4% (Ukrainian); Google Translate accuracy on non-stereotypical roles: 63.5% (Spanish), 48.9% (Russian), 42.9% (Ukrainian); Human annotation agreement: 87%; Inter-annotator agreement: 90%
- All tested MT systems perform significantly better on stereotypical gender roles compared to non-stereotypical roles.
- Introducing stereotypical adjectives like ''handsome'' and ''pretty'' improved translation accuracy and reduced bias in certain languages.
**Notable Facts:**
- The performance difference in F1 score between stereotypical and non-stereotypical roles is consistent across all tested systems.
- Human validation involved 9,600 annotations by native speakers to assess the accuracy of gender bias evaluation methods.

## 6. 5 Conclusions (pages 5-5)
**Overview:** This section presents the conclusions of a study on gender bias in machine translation (MT), highlighting the significant influence of gender stereotypes on translation outputs across multiple languages and systems. It emphasizes the need for further research and development of gender-balanced MT models.
**Key Topics:** Gender bias in machine translation, Evaluation of commercial and academic MT systems, Public availability of data and code
**Key Metrics:** number_of_languages_tested: 8; number_of_MT_systems_evaluated: 6; public_repository: https://github.com/gabrielStanovsky/mt_gender
- All four tested popular commercial MT systems and two state-of-the-art academic models showed significant gender bias.
- Translations were more influenced by gender stereotypes than by meaningful context.
**Notable Facts:**
- This study provides the first large-scale multilingual quantitative evidence for gender bias in MT.
- The research acknowledges contributions from various individuals, including Mark Yatskar and Iz Beltagy, for discussions on gender roles in languages.

## 7. References (pages 6-6)
**Overview:** This section lists various references cited throughout the document, primarily focusing on research related to machine translation, morphological analysis, and gender bias in natural language processing. It serves as a comprehensive bibliography for readers seeking to explore the foundational and contemporary works in these areas.
**Key Topics:** Morphological analysis, Machine translation evaluation, Gender bias in NLP
**Key Metrics:** total_references: 50; years_covered: 1986-2019
- The references include significant contributions to the field from various authors, highlighting advancements in machine translation and morphological analysis.
- Several studies focus on the evaluation and mitigation of gender bias in natural language processing systems.
**Notable Facts:**
- The earliest reference dates back to 1986, while the most recent is from 2019.
- Key conferences mentioned include ACL, EMNLP, NAACL-HLT, and NIPS, indicating the relevance of the cited works in prominent NLP forums.
